issues.body:
Subdomain Validation is a cool concept, and one that I'm happy dns-persist is supporting. In the terms of the Baseline Requirements, this is equivalent to selecting an Authorization Domain Name by pruning labels from the left-hand side of the FQDN, and then performing validation at that ADN.

However, ACME does not have good support for selecting ADNs. Traditional CAs, or CAs which use External Account Binding, might have ways for Applicants to indicate to the Server that they want to use a particular ADN for all of their validation. But plain ACME CAs without EAB would have no way to know whether the Applicant wants to validate at `server.dept.example.com`, at `dept.example.com`, or at `example.com`. I'm not a fan of the server having to fan out a bunch of prospective queries to see what layer of the DNS hierarchy the Applicant has placed their dns-persist record at.

So it would be great if there was a way for the Applicant to proactively indicate to the CA that they want to take advantage of Subdomain Validation. My proposal would be that the ACME client can include an ADN in their POST request to the Challenge URL. The CA would then check that the supplied URL is a valid ADN for the Authorization, and if it is, perform validation at that URL instead. And of course, as already described by the document, ensure that `policy=wildcard` is set.

-----

i.e. if we have an authorization that looks like this:
```
   HTTP/1.1 200 OK
   Content-Type: application/json
   Link: <https://acme.ca/directory>;rel="index"

   {
     "status": "pending",
     "expires": "2026-02-01T14:09:30Z",

     "identifier": {
       "type": "dns",
       "value": "admin.blog.example.com"
     },

     "challenges": [
       {
         "type": "http-01",
         "url": "https://acme.ca/chall/PAniVnsZcis/http-01",
         "token": "DGyRejmCefe7v4NfDGDKfA"
       },
       {
         "type": "tls-alpn-01",
         "url": "https://acme.ca/chall/PAniVnsZcis/tls-alpn-01",
         "token": "DGyRejmCefe7v4NfDGDKfA"
       },
       {
         "type": "dns-01",
         "url": "https://acme.ca/chall/PAniVnsZcis/dns-01",
         "token": "DGyRejmCefe7v4NfDGDKfA"
       },
       {
         "type": "dns-persist-01",
         "url": "https://acme.ca/chall/PAniVnsZcis/dns-persist-01",
         "token": "DGyRejmCefe7v4NfDGDKfA"
       },
     ]
   }
```

Then a client might post this to the challenge url:
```
   POST /chall/PAniVnsZcis/dns-persist-01
   Host: acme.ca
   Content-Type: application/jose+json

   {
     "protected": base64url({
       "alg": "ES256",
       "kid": "https://acme.ca/acct/evOfKhNU60wg",
       "nonce": "UQI1PoRi5OuXzxuX7V7wL0",
       "url": "https://acme.ca/chall/PAniVnsZcis/dns-persist-01"
     }),
     "payload": base64url({
       "adn": "example.com"
     }),
     "signature": "Q1bURgJoEslbD1c5...3pYdSMLio57mQNN4"
   }
```

Where the `"adn": "example.com"` is the important/new part. The CA would confirm that `example.com` is a valid ADN for `admin.blog.example.com`, and then conduct the dns-persist-01 validation at `example.com` and ensure that the record contains the `policy=wildcard` modifier.

comments.body:
> Perhaps a middle ground could be to have the CA send one accounturi which the client knows it could use but put in some language about the CA possibly accepting other accounturi values so long as they appropriately authorize the requesting ACME account.

@birgelee - Coming back to this conversation, I think I like this middle ground proposal more than my original proposal. The reason for that is that I feel this would provide the client some hinting with a default value (probably the ACME account unless configured by the CA to be something different) but at the same time add language that offers CAs some flexibility to accept other values to support other use cases such as External Account Binding scenarios or other enterprise use cases. Forcing all these values to be enumerated and sent back could be burdensome on both the server and the client and not really add much practical value. Especially if the provisioning of these persistent records would typically be handled by an entity other than the client as @sheurich alluded to. 

Let me know your thoughts and I'll be happy to update the language.
